    "result_text": "All green. Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required a new owner's `membershipLevel` to be capped at one above the current maximum among their existing household members, with no cap when there are no existing members. Making the new `coreCapsLevelByHousehold` acceptance test pass required two coordinated changes:\n\n### 1. The membership-level cap (the stated feature)\n- **`Owner` entity** + all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres): added a persisted `membership_level` column, so the capped value survives the round-trip through `GET /api/owners/{id}` (which re-maps from the stored entity).\n- **`OwnerMapper`**: split the level logic \u2014 `deriveMembershipLevel(owner)` computes the base level from points as before; `toMembershipLevel(owner)` now returns the stored capped value when present, else derives it (so seed/unmigrated owners are unaffected).\n- **New `AssignMembershipLevel` function**: computes the derived base level, then caps it to `min(base, maxExistingMemberLevel + 1)` over non-deleted owners sharing the same `householdId`; with no existing member the derived level stands. Wired into `owners.POST.yml` after `assignHouseholdSize` (so the household bonus is settled) and before `save`, under the write transaction.\n\n### 2. Allowing multiple household members (precondition the test exposed)\nThe test creates two owners with the same last name + postcode (same household) but different telephones, and expects **both** to be created. `RejectDuplicateIdentity` was rejecting *any* second owner in a household on `householdId` alone \u2014 directly contradicting the `OwnerIdentity` utility's documented contract that duplicate detection uses the **full identity key** (`telephone|email|householdId`), under which *\"two members of the same household with different telephones... are both allowed.\"* I changed `RejectDuplicateIdentity` to reject only on full-key match, restoring consistency with `OwnerIdentity` and `AssignPossibleDuplicate` (which flags such a second owner as a soft duplicate). Same-key duplicates and the `sharesHousehold` bypass still behave as before.\n\n### Verification\nFull `mvn test` suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The audit log confirms the behavior: owner *a* \u2192 `membershipLevel=1`, owner *b* (has email, base level 2) \u2192 capped result `2` = `min(2, 1+1)`, satisfying `lb \u2264 la + 1`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: change #2 is broader than the one-line spec but is unavoidable \u2014 the test cannot create a second household member otherwise, and the household-only rejection was already inconsistent with the `OwnerIdentity` contract documented in the codebase.",
